From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Wed, 18 May 94 15:09:19 GMT
Subject: [#94-5-219] Orangutangs

 Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu> writes: 
>
>   There are millions of humans who are temporarily or permanently unable
>to pick a lock due to various reasons (too young, too old, too sick, too

Important note: I wasn't just talking about picking a lock, but also
(presumably) coming up with the idea of lock-picking.

>stupid/retarded).

I've been thinking about the same question, but in a rather different
form.....at this point, most people think of everyone human as having
rights, even though many don't especially fulfill any particular
definition for having them. Settling the matter on a genetic basis
simplifies things, and probably not at an excessive cost.

>   Should they have less rights than orangutangs?

Alternatively, if it makes sense to give rights to some orangutangs,
might it make sense to give rights to all orangutangs?

>   Or should we declare them a part of superior race/species anyway,
>contrary to all facts, just because we feel like it?  (do we?)

There might be more to the stricture against denying rights to
all people than feeling like it. It might be worse to deny rights
to large groups of people who get defined as not human than to
extend rights to some who can't use them. Also, a lot of people
get temporarily incapcitated--you might be better off not having
to arrange in advance for defences for yourself in case you're
in a coma than you would be if you had the privilege of killing
anyone in a coma you happen to find.

>   What would you say if somebody, having to choose between two alternatives,
>sacrificed a human baby to save a more intelligent, sensitive, emotional,
>caring and mature orangutang?

I really don't know--I'd be revolted if it was their own baby, though.

If adult orangutangs overlap well into the adult human range, it might
reasonably considered a toss-up.

>   It's not really a new argument, but still a good test for one's ethical
>principles.
> 
>   Transhuman ethics may appear evil...

Or they may appear crazedly generous....

Nancy Lebovitz 

