From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
Date: Wed, 18 May 1994 09:25:10 -0700
Subject: [#94-5-220] Orangutangs

[The First Extropian Squirrel wakes up after a long springtime nap...]

> [Several bold persons reopen the old extropian debate about animal
>  rights, not to say rights in general...]
 
Nancy Lebovitz says:
 
> Settling the matter [of rights, if I read correctly] on a genetic basis
> simplifies things, and probably not at an excessive cost.
 
  I wish to duck the issue of whether there are or ought to be "natural"
rights -- we've been there and back rather unproductively rather often.
However, persons who do advocate assignment of "rights" on some basis
other than at gunpoint (insert half smiley here) often do so on a
behavioral basis  -- to simplfy overmuch, one hears the argument "if they
act human, we'll give 'em human rights".  I'm not sure whether Nancy had
such an approach in mind, and I don't wish to put words in her mouth, but
I would like to point out that to take a behavioral approach toward the
assignment of rights and then to suggest that genetic criteria can settle
the issue of who has rights, is effectively to assert that all behavior is
genetically determined.
  In the strict sense, this latter statement is probably wrong:  Among
animals, at least, a more defensible position might be that the capability
to acquire behavior is genetically determined, but even that one has
possible room for waffling, in that brain development certainly has to do
with nutrition and other conditions that a juvenile animal is subject to.
 
  This line of reasoning leads to interesting statments about "rights" and
other matters, such as "so-and-so wasn't brought up right, so now he isn't
human", which is not too far from the legal position whereby certain
convicted criminals are deprived of some "rights", and "he isn't human now,
but maybe we can fix it by corrective counseling."  In the line of reasoning
about "natural rights" that has them behaviorally based and accepts that
behavior is influenced by environment, each of these statements may have
merit.
 
  And in the context of "animal rights", a related question might indeed be,
"suppose you bring up an animal sufficiently well that it exhibits some
portion of the behavior which, among humans, you would use as criteria for
the assignment of rights; does it then get some rights assigned to it?"

                                               --  Jay Freeman,
                                                   First Extropian Squirrel

PS:  Again, I do not wish to ascribe to Nancy Lebovitz viewpoints which she
     might not hold or might wish to express differently; I am merely using
     her comment as an entry-point into a larger issue.

