From: Mark Grant <Mark.Grant@isltd.insignia.com>
Date: Wed, 18 May 94 19:51:43 BST
Subject: [#94-5-222] Orangutangs

-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman) said :

> I would like to point out that to take a behavioral approach toward the
> assignment of rights and then to suggest that genetic criteria can settle
> the issue of who has rights, is effectively to assert that all behavior is
> genetically determined.

I presumed the intention was to base it on some kind of collection of
genes that distinguish humans from other animals, but in practice I
think that's even worse. Given the amount of garbage in human DNA, I'd
be very suprised if many 'humans' a century from now had DNA remotely
similar to ours, or even to each other.  That's before you consider
engineered animals.

>   And in the context of "animal rights", a related question might indeed be,
> "suppose you bring up an animal sufficiently well that it exhibits some
> portion of the behavior which, among humans, you would use as criteria for
> the assignment of rights; does it then get some rights assigned to it?"

IMHO, if you're going to assign 'rights' to things (and with the
increasing calls for more and more 'rights', I'm not sure the concept
is viable any more), I think that the only way you can do it is based
on behaviour. However, I keep thinking back to the way animals were
reportedly put on trial for crimes in some parts of Europe a few
centuries ago and wonder if it's a good idea in any case... they may
well prefer being able to live outside our legal systems.

			Mark


